\begin_layout Standard
Given the shortcomings of the 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{2}$
\end_inset

, we have argued that the empirical MSE of the poverty estimate at the area
 level represents a better metric for model assessment.
 Panel (a) of Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:mse"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 thus presents our empirical MSE results for several alternative models.
 The first three models apply gradient boosting at the municipality level
 using three alternative covariate sets: geo-reference covariates (
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

GIS-MUN
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

), census-based covariates (
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

CEN-MUN
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

), and then a model with all available covariates (
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

ALL-MUN
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

).
 The fourth model applies gradient boosting at the PSU level and only uses
 census-based covariates (
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

CEN-PSU
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

).
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Recall the the geo-referenced covariates are only available at the municipality
 level.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 The final three models are all traditional methods that we use to benchmark
 the performance of gradient boosting.
 We present unit-level (CensusEB), unit-context, and area level models.
 In particular, the empirical best (CensusEB) model is often considered
 the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

gold standard
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 of small-area estimation and corresponds to an ideal scenario where up-to-date,
 micro-level census data are available.
 The unit-context has been proposed for cases when census data are out-of-date
 although the method has been found deficient in similar validation studies
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citealt
key "corral2022guidelines,corral2021map"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 For off-census years the recommended small area estimation method is the
 Fay-Herriot area-level model.
 The unit-context and Fay-Herriot models are direct competitors to the machine-l
earning models, which are often motivated by a lack of up-to-date census
 data.
 For these traditional models, all available covariates enter the models
 linearly (i.e., we do not include interaction or quadratic terms).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
