 than the aggregate MSE for each sample (i.e., the results are based on Eq.
 [
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:mseg"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

]).
 It is useful to first consider how gradient boosting performs relative
 to the traditional models when using the same census-based covariates.
 As is evident in the results from panel (a) and (b) of Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:mse"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 the XGBoost methods using census aggregate data (Gradient boosting [Census
 agg.
 PSU] and Gradient boosting [Census agg.
 mun]) perform better than unit-context and Fay-Herriot ([Census agg.
 PSU] and [Census agg.
 mun]) models and approximate the performance of CensusEB [CensusEB] estimates.
 The median MSE for the CensusEB model is 0.003 while that of XGBoost methods
 is 0.002 and both methods have similar interquartile ranges.
 The results suggest that XGBoost methods can approximate CensusEB unit-level
 methods and will outperform unit-context and Fay-Herriot methods by a considera
ble margin, which is quite encouraging for cases where estimates are needed
 when the survey and census data are not contemporaneous.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The unit-context models include covariates at the PSU and municipality level.
 The location effects are specified at the municipality level.
 
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now that we have established that in the context of the Mexican data used
 and with covariates derived from census data, XGBoost methods can perform
 just as well as CensusEB methods our attention is turned to XGBoost's performan
ce under geo-referenced data [Gradient Boosting (GIS mun)].
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Note that the underlying model for CensusEB estimates relies on household
 level microdata.
 Consequently, the data requirements for CensusEB is even higher.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 The XGBoost model with GIS-based covariates is our closest approximation
 to the standard implementation of machine learning in the context of poverty
 mapping.
 This model is nevertheless the worst performing of all the models: the
 median MSE for the model with GIS-based covariates is 0.008 (with a root
 MSE of about 0.09), meaning that the median municipality's error is roughly
 nine percentage points.
 In contrast, the CensusEB, unit-context, and Fay-Herriot models fit at
 the PSU level achieve median MSE levels of 0.003, 0.004 and 0.004, with correspond
ing root MSE values of 0.05, 0.06, and 0.06, respectively.
 The Fay-Herriot model fit at the municipal level has a median municipal
 MSE that is slightly higher although the ranges are tighter than those
 of unit-context models and also outperform the GIS based gradient boosting
 model.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
A notable aspect of Fay-Herriot and Gradient Boosting models is their relative
 ease of application and the fact that these usually take considerably less
 time in implementing as opposed to CensusEB and unit-context models.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 While the implementation with geo-referenced data shows relatively poor
 performance, the remaining three XGBoost models that incorporate census-based
 covariates perform much better, with the PSU-level model achieving a median
 MSE of 0.002 and outperforming even CensusEB estimates.
\end_layout

