\begin_layout Standard
It is evident that the performance of XGBoost methods depend heavily on
 the covariates used in the model, with the census-based covariates appearing
 to considerably improve the performance of the model.
 Another way to see that the census-based covariates have greater predictive
 power than the GIS-based covariates is by looking at feature importance.
 The standard measure of feature importance used by XGBoost summarizes the
 gain (i.e., reduction of the loss function) associated with any given feature.
 That is, for a given run of the model, feature importance captures the
 average gain of a given feature, where the gains are averaged across all
 trees in the model.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
In addition, the average gains for a given run of the model are normalized
 by dividing each by the sum of all average gains for that run.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:importance"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 plots the 20 covariates with the highest feature importance after running
 XGBoost with all covariates on each sample and averaging each covariate's
 importance across the 500 samples.
 We find that all of the top 10 covariates are census-based, with only five
 GIS-based covariates entering the top 20.
\end_layout

